    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Published imbibition experiments of an advancing fracture water level surrounding a single matrix block are simulated using various double porosity models. These double porosity formulations are inherently unable to represent transverse imbibition with an advancing water level in the fracture as they are based essentially on one dimensional flow. Gross matches of the experimental and double porosity simulations required shape factors that were not representative of the matrix block size. Increasing the fracture mater injection rate required increasing the shape factor in order to achieve gross matches, hence a constant shape factor does not give the best matches with experimental data at different rates.X new double porosity transfer function for transverse imbibition is presented based on a numerical model for imbibition and the effective exposure time of elements of the matrix block to fracture water. Two different models for imbibition are considered, the first based upon a nonlinear diffusion coefficient and the second being a modified form of the countercurrent imbibition model proposed by Blair (1962). This new double porosity formulation uses the physical dimensions of the matrix block directly, i.e. no shape factor, and was effective in matching the experimental data without tuning any parameters. A comparison of a cubical single matrix undergoing fracture waterflooding was made between the various double porosity formulations and a fine grid, single porosity simulation. The conventional double porosity simulations did not match this fine grid simulation as well as the proposed double porosity formulation when using the countercurrent imbibition model. The nonlinear diffusion equation model for imbibition matched published imbibition experiments but overpredicted the recovery from the fine grid, cubic matrix block simulations. Simulation of a multimatrix block system using the proposed double porosity formulation showed the dependence of the individual block transfer function on the presence of other matrix blocks. The total transfer rate in the multiblock system reached a constant value indicating t'he presence Gf a stabilized saturation zone across the matrix blocks. The proposed multiblock transfer function was also able to match the multiblock fine grid simulation results.",
